+> Care from bariatric surgeons with relevant specialist credentials, with procedures performed at Perth hospital locations confirmed with your surgeon. Verify the credentials and hospital affiliations before publication.
+
+Why it matters: “Leading” is a comparative claim, and the credential wording should not imply a certification that has not been verified.
+
+3. **The risks section calls surgery a “powerful tool”**
+
+> Surgery is a powerful tool, but it works best when supported by the habits and care built around it — it is not a standalone cure.
+
+> Surgery is one treatment option with ongoing eating, nutrition and follow-up requirements. Your surgeon and care team will explain the likely benefits, limits and risks for your circumstances; outcomes vary between individuals.
+
+Why it matters: Keep the “not a cure” clarification without making a broad effectiveness claim.
+
+4. **The cost paragraph promises a significant level of private-health cover**
+
+> The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of every cost that applies to your situation so there are no surprises.
+
+> The [cost of weight loss surgery in Perth](/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth) varies by procedure, hospital, clinician fees, private-health policy, waiting periods, excess and individual circumstances. [Medicare and private-health benefits](/medicare-private-health-cover) may apply only when the relevant requirements are met; ask Medicare and your insurer to confirm what applies. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, and the team should provide an itemised estimate without promising coverage or naming finance providers.
+
+Why it matters: “A significant portion ... is covered” and named funding options can read as a coverage or access promise. Keep the funding explanation measured and consultation-based.
+
+5. **The GP-referral FAQ makes a second rebate and out-of-pocket claim**
+
+> **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?  **
+> You can contact us directly; a GP referral is not required to get started. That said, a referral is worth having as it allows Medicare rebates to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. If you don't have one yet, our team can advise you on next steps when you get in touch.
+
+> **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?** You can contact the practice directly; a GP referral is not required to get started. A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements, but the practice and Medicare should confirm what applies to your appointment and fees.
+
+Why it matters: A referral should not be presented as guaranteed rebate access or a guaranteed reduction in the patient's cost.
+
+6. **The FAQ contains a secondary GLP-1 reference**
