+## Batch-1 gap-closing additions (G1-G5)
+
+### G1 — residual compliance claims
+
+1. **Laparoscopic recovery comparison**
+
+> Most bariatric surgeries in Australia are performed as laparoscopic keyhole surgeries, meaning less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and generally, a faster recovery than open surgery. No procedure is a ‘one-size-fits-all’; your surgeon will spend time understanding your weight loss goals and recommend a procedure based on those, any accompanying health conditions, and your lifestyle.
+
+> Most bariatric surgeries are performed laparoscopically, using small incisions. Your surgeon will explain the technique, expected recovery, alternatives and individual risks, then discuss which procedure may be clinically appropriate for you.
+
+Why it matters: Remove the comparative pain and recovery claim; the remaining explanation is evidence-neutral and does not imply a better outcome.
+
+2. **Gastric bypass comparison and preference claim**
+
+> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
+
+> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): changes stomach size and how food passes through the small intestine. It has procedure-specific nutritional requirements and risks; your surgeon can explain why it may or may not be appropriate for your circumstances.
+
+Why it matters: Remove the comparative weight-loss and “frequently preferred” claims from this hub summary.
+
+3. **Mini bypass comparative complexity and outcome claim**
+
+> Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass): a simplified variation of the Roux-en-Y bypass that creates a single connection between the stomach and small intestine, rather than two. The result is a shorter, less complex operation with a similar weight loss outcome for most patients, though with a slightly different risk profile. Your surgeon can advise whether it's a suitable option for your circumstances.
+
+> Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass): a bypass variation that creates a single connection between the stomach and small intestine. The surgeon can explain its technical features, risks, follow-up needs and whether it is appropriate for your circumstances.
+
+Why it matters: The draft makes comparative complexity and outcome claims that are unnecessary on a hub page.
+
+4. **Gastric-band comparative outcome claim**
+
+> Gastric band (adjustable band): a reversible procedure in which an inflatable silicone band is placed around the upper stomach, creating a small pouch that slows eating and promotes fullness. No cutting or stapling is involved and the band can be adjusted or removed. It generally produces the least weight loss of the surgical options and requires ongoing follow-up for adjustments.
+
+> Gastric band (adjustable band): an implanted adjustable band that can be adjusted or removed, but still requires surgery, ongoing reviews and monitoring for band-related complications. Your surgeon can explain the expected follow-up and whether it is appropriate for you.
+
+Why it matters: Remove the comparative “least weight loss” statement and avoid presenting reversibility as a low-risk shortcut.
+
+5. **Gastric-sleeve suitability and quantified outcome wording**
+
+> Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy): our most common procedure and suitable for a wide range of patients. A large portion of the stomach is removed, leaving a slim tube (the ‘sleeve’) through which food moves much more slowly, and reduces ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’). Many people lose ~60–70% of excess weight, though individual results will vary.
+
+> Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy): removes part of the stomach to create a smaller stomach. The surgeon will explain the expected effects, risks, nutritional needs and whether it is appropriate for you; individual results vary.
+
+Why it matters: Avoid broad suitability wording on the hub and leave procedure-specific outcome discussion to an assessed consultation and the child page.
+
+6. **Residual weight-loss assurance in the risks section**
+
+> Weight regain is possible if lifestyle habits aren't sustained; surgery is a powerful tool but works best alongside ongoing dietary and behavioural support.
+
+> Weight regain remains possible, and follow-up needs vary. Ongoing dietary and behavioural support can help the care team monitor your progress and adjust advice.
+
+Why it matters: Keep the risk disclosure without describing surgery as a “powerful tool” or implying a preferred treatment effect.
+
+7. **Residual comparative sleeve/bypass claim in FAQ 3**
+
+> A sleeve gastrectomy reduces the size of the stomach, leaving digestion otherwise unchanged. A gastric bypass does the same but also reroutes the small intestine, meaning the body absorbs fewer kilojoules from food. The bypass tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and stronger outcomes for type 2 diabetes and reflux, but is a more complex procedure with greater long-term nutritional demands. Your surgeon will help determine which is the better procedure for your situation.
+
