+- **Replace the seven-answer FAQ block:**
+
+  > ### FAQs (7 questions)
+  >
+  > **1. How do I know which bariatric procedure is right for me?** The right procedure depends on your health history, weight loss goals, existing health conditions (like reflux or diabetes), and your lifestyle. There is no universal answer; what works well for one person may not be the best fit for another. This is exactly what your initial consultation is for. Your surgeon will review your circumstances thoroughly and recommend the procedure most likely to deliver safe, sustained results for you specifically.
+  >
+  > **2. Is bariatric surgery available on Medicare or private health insurance?** Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
+  >
+  > **3. What is the difference between a gastric sleeve and a gastric bypass?** A sleeve gastrectomy reduces the size of the stomach, leaving digestion otherwise unchanged. A gastric bypass does the same but also reroutes the small intestine, meaning the body absorbs fewer kilojoules from food. The bypass tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and stronger outcomes for type 2 diabetes and reflux, but is a more complex procedure with greater long-term nutritional demands. Your surgeon will help determine which is the better procedure for your situation.
+  >
+  > **4. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options?** Surgery is not the only path. Medical weight management, including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs, is a genuine and effective option for many people, and our team offers this alongside surgical pathways. Some patients pursue medical management first and decide to progress to surgery later; others find it sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, not a particular procedure.
+  >
