+6. **Residual outcome and comparison claims remain after the first fix**
+
+> Gastric bypass surgery is one of the oldest, most well-known bariatric procedures in Australia, with the Roux-en-Y gastric bypass being the most common and often the preferred option for those managing associated health conditions such as type 2 diabetes or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) in addition to overweight or obesity.
+
+> Gastric bypass surgery is an established bariatric procedure. Roux-en-Y gastric bypass may be discussed when factors such as type 2 diabetes or reflux are part of a patient's health picture, but suitability and expected outcomes vary.
+
+> A gastric bypass also alters your body’s hormones, including the production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’) and blood sugar. This is the main reason the bypass has a strong evidence base for type 2 diabetes management. It’s not uncommon to see blood sugar control/improvement within days of surgery, often even before significant weight loss occurs, though results will vary based on individual circumstances.
+
+> A gastric bypass changes gut hormones involved in hunger, fullness and blood sugar control. Some patients may see blood-sugar changes early after surgery, but the extent, timing and duration of any improvement vary between individuals; your treating doctors will explain what to expect.
+
+> Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 70-80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
+
+> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary and no outcome is guaranteed.
+
+> Often relieves acid reflux and heartburn, unlike some procedures that can worsen it.
+
+> Acid reflux and heartburn may improve for some patients, but outcomes vary and no result is guaranteed.
+
+> Long-term hormonal changes that reduce constant hunger, making healthier eating feel more achievable rather than a daily battle.
+
+> Long-term hormonal changes may affect hunger and fullness for some patients; eating patterns and outcomes vary.
+
+> Improvement in other conditions linked to overweight and obesity, such as high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, and joint pain.
+
+> Weight-related conditions such as high blood pressure, sleep apnoea and joint pain may change after surgery for some patients, but outcomes vary and ongoing medical care may still be needed.
+
+> Dumping syndrome; feeling unwell after eating sugary or fatty foods. Dumping syndrome usually improves as eating habits adjust.
+
+> Dumping syndrome can cause symptoms after eating sugary or fatty foods, and symptoms may change as eating habits adjust. Your dietitian will explain what to watch for and when to seek advice.
+
+> On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.
+
+> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
+
+> Most people adapt well though and many find their relationship with food improves meaningfully over time.
+
+> Eating experiences vary between individuals, and your dietitian will guide the gradual food reintroduction and long-term eating plan.
+
+> Neither procedure is universally better. The bypass tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and has stronger outcomes for type 2 diabetes and acid reflux, but it is more complex with greater nutritional demands long-term. The sleeve is less invasive and leaves your digestion intact. The right choice is an individual one, best made in consultation with your surgeon after a thorough assessment of your health history and goals.
+
+> Neither procedure is universally better. Gastric bypass may be discussed for some patients with type 2 diabetes or reflux, but it is more complex and requires lifelong nutritional follow-up; a gastric sleeve may be appropriate for others. Read our [gastric sleeve Perth guide](/gastric-sleeve-perth) and [bariatric surgery Perth overview](/bariatric-surgery-perth), then discuss suitability, risks and expected outcomes with your surgeon.
