content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:39:> Gastric sleeve surgery cost varies depending on your level of private health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your situation. Medicare item number 31575 does attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon’s fee for a primary laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy is \$4,000 for both insured and uninsured patients, rising to \$5,000 for a complex case. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical fees can be found here.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:41:> Total out-of-pocket costs vary depending on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital charges, complexity and any applicable gap. Medicare item 31575 may attract a rebate when MBS requirements are met, while private health insurance may contribute to hospital and theatre costs depending on the policy, waiting periods and fund rules. Any current fee range should be discussed at consultation, together with the components that apply to your circumstances; ask the team to explain available funding pathways and review the current gastric sleeve cost guide before deciding whether to proceed.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:47:> Gastric sleeve surgery is highly effective for weight loss for two key reasons: first, because your stomach is smaller, you feel fuller, faster. Secondly, because a reduced stomach size minimises your body’s production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’), after gastric sleeve surgery, your appetite will also be reduced.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:51:Why it matters: "Will also be reduced" reads like an outcome guarantee, while the rewrite keeps the mechanism and qualifies individual variation.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:63:> **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the hospital component. A full fee breakdown is available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:65:> **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item 31575 may attract a rebate when the MBS requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every fee. Hospital, theatre, surgeon and anaesthetist costs, private-health benefits and out-of-pocket gaps depend on your circumstances and policy, so confirm current details with your surgeon’s office and insurer. A consultation can help you understand which costs and rebates apply to you.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:73:> It is typically performed ‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier recovery than some more invasive procedures.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:75:> It is typically performed laparoscopically, via keyhole surgery; recovery depends on the procedure and the individual, and your surgeon will provide tailored advice.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:77:> Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:79:> Weight loss may occur after surgery; clinical averages are reported as 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months in some studies, but individual results vary and no result can be guaranteed.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:85:> Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery time) than open surgery.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:87:> Keyhole surgery uses smaller incisions than open surgery; scarring, pain, hospital stay and recovery vary by individual and surgical circumstances.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:89:> Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:93:> Staple line leak; though it is a serious potential complication and may require further treatment, it only occurs in ~1–3% of cases.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:107:> **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size without rerouting the small intestine. A gastric bypass reduces stomach size and reroutes part of the small intestine, so the procedures can have different nutritional and weight-loss effects. Reflux history, BMI, other health conditions and surgeon assessment all contribute to the choice; neither procedure is right for everyone.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:111:> **6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right for me?** Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon because a sleeve can worsen symptoms in some patients. A bypass may be discussed as an alternative for some people with significant GORD, but the choice requires individual assessment of reflux, BMI, other health conditions and surgical risk.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:113:Why it matters: These phrases imply guaranteed benefit, minimise risk, or make comparative safety/effectiveness claims. The replacements keep the information useful while making outcomes and recovery individual and measured.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:119:> If you are one of the [<u>75% of West Australians</u>](https://www.health.wa.gov.au/~/media/Corp/Documents/Health-for/Nutrition-monitoring-survey-series/2022/Weight-and-Health_FINAL.pdf) living with overweight or obesity and are exploring different types of bariatric surgery, you may be considering a sleeve gastrectomy (gastric sleeve surgery).
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:121:> If you are researching gastric sleeve Perth options after years of weight loss and regain, this guide explains the procedure, costs, Medicare considerations, risks, recovery and how OSWA assesses whether surgery is suitable for you.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:123:Why it matters: Use the H1 "Gastric Sleeve Perth: Surgery, Cost, Eligibility and Recovery" and a title tag such as "Gastric Sleeve Perth: Cost & Recovery | OSWA" so the exact primary keyword appears in the title, H1 and first 100 words.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:143:> For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:151:> A gastric sleeve permanently reduces stomach size, a gastric bypass reduces stomach size and reroutes part of the small intestine, and prescription weight-management medicines, including GLP-1 medicines, work differently again; there is no single best option for everyone, and your surgeon can explain how reflux, BMI, diabetes, medication history and surgical risk affect the choice.
