content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:36:The SERP is almost entirely commercial: individual Perth bariatric surgeon and clinic homepages (Dr Siva Gounder / perthweightlosssurgery.com.au, perthbariatricsurgery.com.au / Dr Andrew Kiyingi, Advance Surgical / Jon Armstrong, Aurora Bariatrics / Dr Ruth Blackham, Dr Kevin Dolan, Perth Surgical & Bariatrics, Evolve Bariatrics). Domain ratings are low across the board (DR 1-26), so this is a winnable, weakly-defended SERP. Almost every result is a surgeon-led homepage selling a procedure menu — not informational guides. People Also Ask boxes surface clear informational intent: "How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?", "Is weight loss surgery covered by Medicare in Australia?", and "What BMI is used to assess weight loss surgery?". Intent is mixed commercial + informational, which fits the brief's positioning of this as the broad top-of-funnel page.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:48:Every top result is a surgeon homepage that funnels straight to surgery — none present medical management (GLP-1 medication, VLCD, dietitian support) as an equal pathway. That is OSWA's biggest differentiation opportunity and aligns exactly with the brief's mandate to frame obesity as a medical condition and not over-index on surgery. Second gap: the PAA boxes show high demand for cost, Medicare coverage, and BMI/eligibility answers, yet competitor homepages bury or omit these — directly addressing how medical management is assessed from BMI 27+, how Medicare rebates and MBS thresholds work, and how much treatment may cost / whether superannuation access can be explored in plain language (the brief already mandates an eligibility section, cost paragraph, and FAQs) will capture featured-snippet and PAA real estate competitors leave open. Third gap: low-DR competitors mean strong topical depth, empathetic non-judgemental framing, and a team-based (dedicated APD dietitian team, physicians + surgeons, 2 clinics) angle can outrank them without heavy backlinks. Note OSWA (obesitysurgerywa.com.au) currently sits at position 5 with DR 11, so this page is an existing-asset improvement play, not a cold start.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:60:Use these factual anchors. Keep the BMI stat explicitly assessment-only; it is not a surgical MBS threshold:
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:67:| Medical-team assessment from | BMI 27+ | Individual assessment; not a surgical MBS threshold |
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:76:- Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Give the reader a quick mental model of the spectrum: medical management (GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support) at one end, surgical procedures (sleeve, bypass, band) at the other. Acknowledge that many people move between them over time, and there is no single correct starting point.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:79:- BMI summary to anchor the section: "You do not need a very high BMI to start a conversation. A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. For the Medicare-listed surgical pathway, current MBS thresholds refer to BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity. We will help you understand what needs to be assessed."
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:81:  - BMI around 27+ with a condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea: invite an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:82:  - Current MBS surgical thresholds: BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity; clinical suitability is assessed individually
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:83:  - Higher BMI with prior attempts at weight loss that did not hold: surgical and non-surgical options can be assessed together
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:109:- Intro paragraph: Acknowledge that every option on the spectrum carries its own considerations. Frame this as a genuine service to the reader — the team will not pretend otherwise. Medications have side effects. Very low calorie diets need supervision. Surgery is a significant step with real risks and recovery. The clinician should explain how these considerations relate to the reader's medical situation before any commitment.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:111:  - GLP-1 medications can cause nausea, digestive symptoms and other side effects; not suitable for everyone
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:144:- Medical advertising: no guarantees, use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "your clinician will advise", "many people experience"
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:145:- AHPRA/TGA: no testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:146:- MBS surgical threshold: state BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity as assessment guidance; BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Do not present a lower-BMI statement as surgical eligibility.
