content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:97:> 1. Initial enquiry and GP referral: most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. Consultation with your surgeon: your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. Wider team assessment: this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. Pre-surgery preparation: you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. Procedure day: surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. Long-term aftercare: follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:103:> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70-80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:111:> Obesity Surgery WA has been a trusted name in Perth weight loss for over 20 years and offers gastric sleeve surgery, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band, and revision surgery at Mount Private Hospital, The Park Private Hospital, and Waikiki Private Hospital.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:113:> Obesity Surgery WA offers [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [mini gastric bypass](/mini-gastric-bypass), [gastric band surgery](/gastric-band), [revision bariatric surgery](/revision-bariatric-surgery) and [non-surgical weight management](/non-surgical-weight-loss). See [eligibility](/am-i-eligible), [Medicare and private health cover](/medicare-private-health-cover), [GP referral](/gp-referral), [surgeons](/surgeons), [FAQs](/faqs) and [book a consultation](/book-consultation).
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:129:> FAQs: 1. How much does bariatric surgery cost in Perth? 2. Is bariatric surgery covered by Medicare or private health insurance? 3. How do I know which procedure is right for me? 4. What is the difference between gastric sleeve and gastric bypass? 5. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options? 6. What is the 30/30 rule after bariatric surgery? 7. Who may not be eligible for bariatric surgery? 8. How should I choose a bariatric surgeon in Perth? Use the answer to question 8 to discuss credentials, FRACS/specialist registration, hospital access, aftercare and comfort with the team - do not claim OSWA is the best.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:131:Why it matters: The current search set includes cost in Perth, best bariatric surgeon in Perth, the 30/30 eating rule, who is not eligible, sleeve-vs-bypass or medication comparisons and long-term outcomes.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:135:> Your surgeon will review your circumstances thoroughly and recommend the procedure most likely to deliver safe, sustained results for you specifically.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:139:Why it matters: This sentence implies the clinician can identify the procedure most likely to deliver a safe sustained result, which is stronger than "your surgeon will advise" and "results vary".
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:171:> Obesity Surgery WA has been a trusted name in Perth weight loss for over 20 years and offers gastric sleeve surgery, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band, and revision surgery at Mount Private Hospital, The Park Private Hospital, and Waikiki Private Hospital.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:193:1. **Laparoscopic recovery comparison**
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:195:> Most bariatric surgeries in Australia are performed as laparoscopic keyhole surgeries, meaning less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and generally, a faster recovery than open surgery. No procedure is a ‘one-size-fits-all’; your surgeon will spend time understanding your weight loss goals and recommend a procedure based on those, any accompanying health conditions, and your lifestyle.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:197:> Most bariatric surgeries are performed laparoscopically, using small incisions. Your surgeon will explain the technique, expected recovery, alternatives and individual risks, then discuss which procedure may be clinically appropriate for you.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:199:Why it matters: Remove the comparative pain and recovery claim; the remaining explanation is evidence-neutral and does not imply a better outcome.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:203:> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:227:> Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy): our most common procedure and suitable for a wide range of patients. A large portion of the stomach is removed, leaving a slim tube (the ‘sleeve’) through which food moves much more slowly, and reduces ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’). Many people lose ~60–70% of excess weight, though individual results will vary.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:257:9. **GLP-1 references: cross-reference plus additional metadata occurrences**
