content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:275:### G2 — MBS-inaccurate BMI wording
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:279:> Bariatric surgery is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 40 or higher, or in specific circumstances for those with a BMI of 35 or higher when an obesity-related health condition is also present. People with a BMI between 30 and 35 may be candidates in specific circumstances, particularly where type 2 diabetes is poorly controlled.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:281:> BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold; discuss any other clinical pathway with your surgeon.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:283:2. **BMI 40+**
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:285:> BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option to discuss, regardless of other conditions
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:287:> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:289:3. **BMI 35-40**
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:291:> BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly considered
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:293:> BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:295:4. **BMI 30-35**
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:297:> BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed on an individual basis
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:299:> BMI below 35: this does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; discuss any non-MBS clinical options with the surgeon, who will assess suitability individually.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:301:Why it matters: The MBS pathway is BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. Nothing below BMI 35 qualifies under MBS, and all BMI wording must remain assessment-only.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:314:  > **Pre-surgery preparation** Your surgeon will explain your procedure and individual recovery plan, and you may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink your liver and reduce surgical risk (typically 2–4 weeks). But you won’t do it alone! Our staff will support you throughout.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:316:  > **Procedure day** Obesity Surgery WA’s bariatric surgery is performed via keyhole surgery in most cases, with short post-operative hospital stays (1-3 nights) at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Guided by nursing and dietetic staff, you’ll start on fluids and your recovery will be monitored until you’re ready to return home.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:322:  > 1. **Initial enquiry and GP referral:** most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. **Consultation with your surgeon:** your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. **Wider team assessment:** this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. **Pre-surgery preparation:** you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. **Procedure day:** surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. **Long-term aftercare:** follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:326:  > **Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy)**: our most common procedure and suitable for a wide range of patients. A large portion of the stomach is removed, leaving a slim tube (the ‘sleeve’) through which food moves much more slowly, and reduces ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’). Many people lose ~60–70% of excess weight, though individual results will vary.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:328:  > **Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)**: restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:336:  > **Medical weight management**: non-surgical weight loss treatment pathways including GLP-1 medications (such as semaglutide) and structured dietitian-led programs. Suits people who do not want surgery, are not yet candidates, or who would like specialist medical support before or instead of proceeding with a surgical procedure.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:349:  > Every surgical procedure carries risk, though modern keyhole techniques and highly experienced surgeons make bariatric procedures safe for most suitable candidates. Below are some of the most common risks, potential complications, and side effects of undergoing bariatric surgery:
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:375:  > **1. How do I know which bariatric procedure is right for me?** The right procedure depends on your health history, weight loss goals, existing health conditions (like reflux or diabetes), and your lifestyle. There is no universal answer; what works well for one person may not be the best fit for another. This is exactly what your initial consultation is for. Your surgeon will review your circumstances thoroughly and recommend the procedure most likely to deliver safe, sustained results for you specifically.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:381:  > **4. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options?** Surgery is not the only path. Medical weight management, including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs, is a genuine and effective option for many people, and our team offers this alongside surgical pathways. Some patients pursue medical management first and decide to progress to surgery later; others find it sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, not a particular procedure.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:385:  > **6. Where will my surgery and appointments take place?** Consultations take place at our clinics in Booragoon or Greenfields, with surgeries performed at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Once your surgery is planned, our team will walk you through exactly what to expect, where to go, and who will be involved at every stage, so there are no surprises on the day.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:393:> Obesity Surgery WA offers [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [mini gastric bypass](/mini-gastric-bypass), [gastric band surgery](/gastric-band), [revision bariatric surgery](/revision-bariatric-surgery) and [non-surgical weight management](/non-surgical-weight-loss). See [eligibility](/am-i-eligible), [Medicare and private health cover](/medicare-private-health-cover), [GP referral](/gp-referral), [surgeons](/surgeons), [FAQs](/faqs) and [book a consultation](/book-consultation).
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:407:> Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but eligibility is assessed individually. Verify the planned item number: 31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch. Private-health benefits depend on the fund, policy, waiting periods and exclusions.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:411:> The choice depends on your medical history, eating patterns, health conditions, goals and willingness to follow the required aftercare. Your surgeon should explain likely benefits, limits, risks and monitoring needs for each option; no procedure or outcome is guaranteed.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:423:> Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:425:> **7. Who may not be eligible for bariatric surgery?**
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:427:> No online checklist can decide suitability. For MBS items, a BMI below 35 does not meet the threshold, and BMI 35–39.9 without at least one major obesity-related condition also does not meet the threshold; a procedure may also be deferred if assessment identifies unacceptable surgical or anaesthetic risk, unresolved medical issues or difficulty completing required follow-up. Your surgeon makes an individual assessment.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:435:> Appointments take place at the confirmed OSWA clinic locations, and surgery takes place at the hospital selected with your surgeon. Hospital names and affiliations must be verified before publication; the team should confirm the location, admission process and follow-up arrangements during planning.
