content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:5:**Why:** Medicare/item-number accuracy, BMI wording, risk disclosure, procedure-specific scaffolding and search coverage need correction before publication.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:13:> Medicare item numbers 31572 and 31581 attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:15:> Medicare rebates may apply when MBS criteria are met. For Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, the relevant MBS item is 31572; your surgeon will confirm the appropriate Medicare item number, rebate eligibility and any private health fund requirements before surgery. Private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover may contribute to hospital and theatre costs, but out-of-pocket costs vary by fund and policy.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:17:Why it matters: Item 31572 is the Roux-en-Y gastric bypass item, while 31581 is for biliopancreatic diversion with or without duodenal switch, so listing both as if they apply to this page is a compliance risk.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:19:2. **BMI eligibility is broader than current MBS wording [also fix the brief]**
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:21:> A gastric bypass is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30-35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:23:> Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, MBS criteria generally describe clinically severe obesity as a BMI of 40 or higher, or a BMI of 35 or higher with a major medical comorbidity. A BMI below those thresholds does not meet the MBS eligibility criteria; your surgeon will consider your full health picture and confirm whether any other clinical or rebate rule applies.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:25:Why it matters: MBS explanatory note TN.8.29 generally describes clinically severe obesity as BMI 40 or higher, or BMI 35 or higher with major medical comorbidities; the writer followed the brief as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:27:3. **"Generally safe" is an unbalanced safety claim**
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:29:> Gastric bypass surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:33:Why it matters: "Generally safe" can underplay the seriousness of the operation unless the sentence gives a fuller risk picture in the same breath.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:35:4. **The diabetes medication claim misses the required doctor-review qualifier**
