content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:45:> Compared to a gastric sleeve, which simply restricts the flow of food through your digestive system, the bypass often results in greater weight loss and better outcomes, especially for those living with type 2 diabetes and acid reflux.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:49:Why it matters: "Better outcomes" is too broad for health advertising unless the claim is balanced with complexity, risk and individual suitability.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:61:> Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 70-80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:63:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary and no outcome is guaranteed.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:67:> Acid reflux and heartburn may improve for some patients, but outcomes vary and no result is guaranteed.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:81:> On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:83:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:95:GLP-1 scan: no GLP-1, semaglutide, liraglutide or brand-name medication reference appears in this draft. The site-wide GLP-1/medical-weight-management advertising policy remains pending; do not add such references until that decision is made.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:109:> A referral is not required to make an enquiry or book a consultation. A valid referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to Medicare rules; it does not guarantee a rebate or cover the full fee. It can also help your surgeon receive relevant medical history, and the team can advise you if you do not yet have one.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:131:> Gastric bypass Perth: surgery, cost, recovery and suitability
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:166:> | gastric bypass surgery recovery time | 100 | 22 |
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:172:> Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, bariatric surgery, laparoscopic / keyhole surgery, stomach pouch, small intestine rerouting, type 2 diabetes remission, gut hormones, ghrelin, blood sugar control, excess weight loss, acid reflux / GORD, dumping syndrome, vitamin and mineral supplements, BMI eligibility, Medicare rebate, private health cover, St John of God Murdoch, anastomotic leak, general anaesthetic, dietitian follow-up.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:178:> Gastric bypass surgery cost in Australia varies depending on your level of private health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your situation. [Medicare item numbers 31572 and 31581](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/search.cfm?q=gastric+bypass&Submit=&sopt=S) attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon's fee for a primary laparoscopic gastric bypass is $6,000 for both insured and uninsured patients, rising to $[X,XXX] for a complex case. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical fees can be found here.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:184:> On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:186:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:202:> Most patients require around 2-3 weeks off desk-based or sedentary work. Physical or manual labour typically requires closer to 6-8 weeks before returning. Everyone recovers at a different pace, though, and your surgeon will provide guidance tailored to your situation. It's worth factoring recovery time into your planning early, particularly if your role involves lifting, physical exertion, or extended periods on your feet.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:204:> Many people need around 2–3 weeks away from desk-based work, while physical or manual work may require longer. Your surgeon will advise based on your recovery and job demands, especially if your role involves lifting, physical exertion or prolonged periods on your feet.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:208:> A gastric bypass is one of the most effective bariatric procedures available, but results depend on a combination of surgery and sustained lifestyle change. If weight loss is slower than expected or regain occurs over time, the first step is reviewing diet, habits, and follow-up care with your team. Further surgical options may be considered in some cases. Your surgeon and dietitian are there to support you through challenges, not just the initial recovery.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:222:> A referral is not required to make an enquiry or book a consultation. A valid referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to Medicare rules; it does not guarantee a rebate or cover the full fee. It can also help your surgeon receive relevant medical history, and the team can advise you if you do not yet have one.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:226:> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at three locations: [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Pre- and post-operative (once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
content-reviews/gastric-bypass-perth.md:246:> Expected weight loss: 70-80% of excess weight, clinical average; results vary. Time in surgery: about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique. Hospital stay: usually 2-3 nights. Return to most activity: about 3-4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later.
