docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:145:> A surgical eligibility assessment gives you a personalised answer based on BMI, weight-related health conditions, previous weight loss attempts, medical risk, readiness for aftercare, and your goals.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:166:Summary: The core medical structure is strong, but revise BMI/mental-health/safety claims into assessment-criteria language and add a direct "who is not eligible" answer.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:171:Source line references below refer to `docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/extracted/am-i-eligible.md`.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:175:1. **The FAQ repeats an unqualified mental-health assurance and an outcome promise**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:177:> Draft L251-L256: **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?  **
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:180:> **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?** A mental health history does not automatically rule out surgery. Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment, and active or unstable concerns, an untreated eating disorder, substance misuse or difficulty following aftercare may affect timing or suitability. Your surgeon will discuss what support or further review may be needed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:188:> Care from bariatric surgeons with relevant specialist credentials, with procedures performed at Perth hospital locations confirmed with your surgeon. Verify the credentials and hospital affiliations before publication.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:213:> **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?** You can contact the practice directly; a GP referral is not required to get started. A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements, but the practice and Medicare should confirm what applies to your appointment and fees.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:215:Why it matters: A referral should not be presented as guaranteed rebate access or a guaranteed reduction in the patient's cost.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:217:6. **The FAQ contains a secondary GLP-1 reference**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:220:> You will not leave without options. If surgery is not the right fit — due to timing, preference, or for clinical reasons — we can discuss medical weight management pathways including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs. Some patients start there and revisit surgery later; others find it is sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, whatever form that takes.</span>
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:224:Why it matters: This is the second explicit GLP-1 occurrence after the first-pass fix. The replacement answers who may not be suitable without implying that every reader will find a satisfactory pathway.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:226:### G2 — MBS-inaccurate BMI wording
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:228:1. **The summary incorrectly places BMI 30-34 on a qualification pathway**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:230:> Draft L84: As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:232:> MBS eligibility is assessed individually. The relevant thresholds are BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your wider health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:234:Why it matters: “May qualify” is still self-qualification language, and the MBS rule does not create eligibility below BMI 35.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:236:2. **The BMI 30-34 bullet implies surgery may be appropriate under MBS**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:238:> Draft L92-L95: BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:240:> BMI 30–34 with a weight-related health condition: this range does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your broader clinical picture and discuss non-MBS options where appropriate; a condition below BMI 35 does not by itself establish eligibility for MBS-funded surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:242:Why it matters: A major comorbidity is relevant to the MBS pathway at BMI 35–39.9; BMI 40 or above is the other threshold limb and does not require a comorbidity, subject to individual clinical assessment.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:244:3. **The BMI 35-39 bullet says other conditions are unnecessary**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:246:> Draft L97-L100: BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the options most appropriate for your situation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:248:> BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. Your surgeon will assess your medical history, risks, goals and aftercare readiness before discussing options.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:250:Why it matters: This is the known G2 error. The BMI 40 or above limb must not be omitted when describing the comorbidity requirement.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:252:4. **The BMI 40+ bullet turns an assessment indicator into an eligibility and effectiveness claim**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:254:> Draft L102-L105: BMI 40+: almost everyone in this range is eligible for surgery and for many patients it represents the most effective long-term pathway available. Your surgeon can confirm which procedure is the right fit.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:256:> BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility or a particular procedure; your surgeon will discuss whether surgery or another pathway is appropriate for you.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:258:Why it matters: BMI 40+ is an MBS indicator, not a promise of eligibility or comparative effectiveness.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:260:5. **The comorbidity bullet says a condition can strengthen a case regardless of BMI**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:262:> Draft L107-L111: Weight-related health conditions or comorbidities: certain conditions strengthen a surgical case regardless of BMI. These include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH), and severe acid reflux.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:264:> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35–39.9, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility below BMI 35. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:266:Why it matters: “Regardless of BMI” conflicts with the MBS threshold and should not appear on an eligibility page.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:268:6. **The BMI 30 FAQ repeats the qualification error**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:270:> Draft L233-L240: **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?  **
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:271:> Not necessarily. A BMI of 30-34 may qualify for a surgical pathway when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present. If surgery is not the right starting point, our medical team can offer meaningful support through medication and dietitian-led care. The only way to know where you stand is a proper assessment.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:273:> **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?** A BMI of 30-34 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your wider clinical picture and discuss non-surgical medical weight management or dietitian-led care where appropriate; an individual assessment is still required.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:275:Why it matters: The FAQ must answer the reader directly without implying that a comorbidity creates an MBS pathway below BMI 35.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:283:  > Draft L1-L42: **05_Am I Eligible for Weight Loss Surgery?** **URL:** /am-i-eligible **Primary keyword:** am i eligible for weight loss surgery **Target word count:** 1,000–1,300 words **Repositioned per client (June 2026):** medical eligibility, moved out of the finance cluster. "Am I eligible?" is a CLINICAL question (BMI, comorbidities, prior attempts, individual assessment), not a financial/affordability one — frame it medically throughout. Keep internal links cost → eligibility → book-consultation intact. **Audience:** This is primarily an ACTIVATION/CONVERSION page for anxious, research-stage patients who are already on-site — not an organic-traffic play. Optimise for reassurance and funnel progression (toward booking the consultation), not for raw search volume. ### Secondary Keywords (target naturally in body copy) | Keyword | AU Vol/mo | KD | |----|----|----| | gastric sleeve surgery eligibility | 20 | 0 | | gastric sleeve perth eligibility | 10 | 0 | | weight loss surgery requirements | 10 | 0 | | do i qualify for weight loss surgery quiz | 10 | 0 | | weight loss surgery medicare | 20 | 0 | | ~~weight loss surgery perth~~ | 250 | 45 | | gastric sleeve perth | 150 | 7 | | weight loss surgery cost | 350 | 0 | ### Semantic Terms to Include bariatric surgery, gastric sleeve, sleeve gastrectomy, ~~BMI (body mass index)~~, ~~class 2 obesity, class 3 obesit~~y~~, comorbidities, type 2 diabetes, obstructive sleep apnoea, high blood pressure, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD)~~, FRACS surgeon, GP referral, Medicare rebate, private health insurance, superannuation early release, dietitian, psychological assessment, orientation consultation, St John of God Murdoch, Subiaco, metabolic surgery
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:285:  > Delete entirely. The publishable copy starts at “Am I eligible for weight loss surgery in Perth?”
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:323:> Draft L284-L291: **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?** You will not leave without options. If surgery is not the right fit — due to timing, preference, or for clinical reasons — we can discuss medical weight management pathways including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs. Some patients start there and revisit surgery later; others find it is sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, whatever form that takes.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:331:> **9. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery?** Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every cost and no rebate should be assumed. The applicable item depends on the procedure—31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch—so your surgeon and insurer should confirm what applies.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:333:> **10. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?** Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:343:> You can [contact the practice](/book-consultation) directly or ask your GP for a [referral](/gp-referral). A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements; ask Medicare and the practice what applies.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:389:- Replaces “BMI 35+ — Usually eligible” with the canonical wording “BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment”; BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:390:- Removes the brief’s “may qualify”, “with or without other health conditions”, “almost everyone eligible” and “most effective long-term option” directions.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:391:- Replaces “surgery is safe” with an assessment of appropriateness, risks and preparation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:394:- Adds the pending TGA/legal policy note: prefer neutral non-surgical medical weight-management wording; if active-ingredient/class wording is retained after review, use semaglutide, liraglutide or GLP-1 only and never brand names.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:398:- **G1:** 6 residual claim groups added at Draft L172-L174, L202-L204, L208-L216, L251-L256, L258-L264 and L284-L291; the existing first-pass mental-health, cost and GLP-1 fixes remain in place.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:399:- **G2:** 6 BMI/comorbidity blocks reframed at Draft L84, L92-L95, L97-L100, L102-L105, L107-L111 and L233-L240. The known L97 G2 example is explicitly corrected.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:404:Verification flags: hospital identity and affiliations remain **to be verified with the client**; the GLP-1/medical-weight-management advertising policy remains pending; confirm current MBS, referral, Medicare and insurer details before publication.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:3:Reason: Medicare/item-number accuracy, BMI wording, risk disclosure, procedure-specific scaffolding and search coverage need correction before publication.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:11:> Medicare item numbers 31572 and 31581 attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:13:> Medicare rebates may apply when MBS criteria are met. For Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, the relevant MBS item is 31572; your surgeon will confirm the appropriate Medicare item number, rebate eligibility and any private health fund requirements before surgery. Private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover may contribute to hospital and theatre costs, but out-of-pocket costs vary by fund and policy.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:15:Why it matters: Item 31572 is the Roux-en-Y gastric bypass item, while 31581 is for biliopancreatic diversion with or without duodenal switch, so listing both as if they apply to this page is a compliance risk.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:17:2. **BMI eligibility is broader than current MBS wording [also fix the brief]**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:21:> A gastric bypass is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30-35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:23:> Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, MBS criteria generally describe clinically severe obesity as a BMI of 40 or higher, or a BMI of 35 or higher with a major medical comorbidity. A BMI below those thresholds does not meet the MBS eligibility criteria; your surgeon will consider your full health picture and confirm whether any other clinical or rebate rule applies.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:25:Why it matters: MBS explanatory note TN.8.29 generally describes clinically severe obesity as BMI 40 or higher, or BMI 35 or higher with major medical comorbidities; the writer followed the brief as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:27:3. **"Generally safe" is an unbalanced safety claim**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:29:> Gastric bypass surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:33:Why it matters: "Generally safe" can underplay the seriousness of the operation unless the sentence gives a fuller risk picture in the same breath.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:35:4. **The diabetes medication claim misses the required doctor-review qualifier**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:43:5. **The comparison copy uses a broad "better outcomes" claim**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:47:> Compared to a gastric sleeve, which simply restricts the flow of food through your digestive system, the bypass often results in greater weight loss and better outcomes, especially for those living with type 2 diabetes and acid reflux.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:51:Why it matters: "Better outcomes" is too broad for health advertising unless the claim is balanced with complexity, risk and individual suitability.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:69:> Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 70-80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:71:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary and no outcome is guaranteed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:75:> Acid reflux and heartburn may improve for some patients, but outcomes vary and no result is guaranteed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:95:> On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:97:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:113:GLP-1 scan: no GLP-1, semaglutide, liraglutide or brand-name medication reference appears in this extracted draft. The site-wide GLP-1/medical-weight-management advertising policy remains pending; do not add such references until that decision is made.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:133:> A referral is not required to make an enquiry or book a consultation. A valid referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to Medicare rules; it does not guarantee a rebate or cover the full fee. It can also help your surgeon receive relevant medical history, and the team can advise you if you do not yet have one.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:157:> Gastric bypass Perth: surgery, cost, recovery and suitability
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:194:> | gastric bypass surgery recovery time | 100 | 22 |
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:200:> Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, bariatric surgery, laparoscopic / keyhole surgery, stomach pouch, small intestine rerouting, type 2 diabetes remission, gut hormones, ghrelin, blood sugar control, excess weight loss, acid reflux / GORD, dumping syndrome, vitamin and mineral supplements, BMI eligibility, Medicare rebate, private health cover, St John of God Murdoch, anastomotic leak, general anaesthetic, dietitian follow-up.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:206:> Gastric bypass surgery cost in Australia varies depending on your level of private health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your situation. [Medicare item numbers 31572 and 31581](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/search.cfm?q=gastric+bypass&Submit=&sopt=S) attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon's fee for a primary laparoscopic gastric bypass is $6,000 for both insured and uninsured patients, rising to $[X,XXX] for a complex case. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical fees can be found here.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:212:> On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:214:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:230:> Most patients require around 2-3 weeks off desk-based or sedentary work. Physical or manual labour typically requires closer to 6-8 weeks before returning. Everyone recovers at a different pace, though, and your surgeon will provide guidance tailored to your situation. It's worth factoring recovery time into your planning early, particularly if your role involves lifting, physical exertion, or extended periods on your feet.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:232:> Many people need around 2–3 weeks away from desk-based work, while physical or manual work may require longer. Your surgeon will advise based on your recovery and job demands, especially if your role involves lifting, physical exertion or prolonged periods on your feet.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:236:> A gastric bypass is one of the most effective bariatric procedures available, but results depend on a combination of surgery and sustained lifestyle change. If weight loss is slower than expected or regain occurs over time, the first step is reviewing diet, habits, and follow-up care with your team. Further surgical options may be considered in some cases. Your surgeon and dietitian are there to support you through challenges, not just the initial recovery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:250:> A referral is not required to make an enquiry or book a consultation. A valid referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to Medicare rules; it does not guarantee a rebate or cover the full fee. It can also help your surgeon receive relevant medical history, and the team can advise you if you do not yet have one.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:254:> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at three locations: [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Pre- and post-operative (once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:274:> Expected weight loss: 70-80% of excess weight, clinical average; results vary. Time in surgery: about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique. Hospital stay: usually 2-3 nights. Return to most activity: about 3-4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:278:6. **Hospital names must be verified with the client before changing either direction**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:280:> After your bypass you'll spend 2-3 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:284:Why it matters: The hospital names conflict with the brief, but the draft may reflect newer client information, so verify the correct hospital name(s) with the client before changing either direction.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:292:> Your GP referral is the first formal step because it helps your surgeon understand your medical history and may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to Medicare rules. You can still contact the practice before you have a referral, and the team can explain what to ask your GP for.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:298:> Your surgeon's fee for a primary laparoscopic gastric bypass is $6,000 for both insured and uninsured patients, rising to $[X,XXX] for a complex case.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:340:> FAQs (13 questions): retain the current high-value questions and add direct answers for "What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?", "Can gastric bypass be covered by Medicare?", "How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?", "Who is not eligible for gastric bypass?", and "What are gastric bypass outcomes after 10 years?" Keep answers to 2-3 sentences and remove or shorten lower-priority duplication to stay within 1,200-1,500 words.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:360:> **Can gastric bypass be covered by Medicare?** For Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, MBS item 31572 may apply when the relevant criteria are met, but Medicare does not usually cover every cost. Your surgeon can confirm whether an MBS item applies; your private health fund can confirm hospital-cover requirements, waiting periods and policy exclusions.
