# Gastric Sleeve Perth — content review
**Length:** current body 1,844 words vs brief target 1,200-1,500 words, including FAQs.
**Verdict: Rework** · 8 must-fix · 12 should-fix · 4 minor
**Why:** Safety, Medicare eligibility, hospital verification, cost certainty and outcome-claim issues need correction before publication.
- **GLP-1 / medical-weight-management advertising policy — PENDING TGA/legal decision:** use only active-ingredient or class names such as semaglutide, liraglutide and GLP-1; do not add brand names, guarantees or comparative claims until the policy position is confirmed. There are no secondary GLP-1/medication occurrences to fix.
> ### **Conversion CTA headline**
> **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with significant reflux better.
> **6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right for me?** Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon, as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.
> **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the hospital component. A full fee breakdown is available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.
> **How much does a gastric sleeve cost in Perth?**
> Another key difference between the gastric sleeve and gastric band is that the sleeve restricts only the amount of food you can consume, not the types of foods (as is the case with the gastric band procedure).
> For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients.
> Gastric sleeve surgery is highly effective for weight loss for two key reasons: first, because your stomach is smaller, you feel fuller, faster. Secondly, because a reduced stomach size minimises your body’s production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’), after gastric sleeve surgery, your appetite will also be reduced.
> Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
> Hair thinning in the months after surgery is typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake stabilises.
> It is typically performed ‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier recovery than some more invasive procedures.
> Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery time) than open surgery.
> Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller portions feel more satisfying.
> Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles, though for others it can be persistent.
> Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
> Single permanent procedure; no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.
