docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:15:Why it matters: TGA states that "advertising prescription medicines to the public is prohibited" and specifically calls out weight-loss injections in compliance action, so naming semaglutide on a promotional service page is a publish risk: https://www.tga.gov.au/news/media-releases/tga-releases-new-guidance-advertising-restrictions-prescription-medicines.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:87:> 20+ yrs bariatric experience | Current hospital locations to be verified | APD dietitian-led aftercare | 4+ surgical pathways including sleeve, bypass, mini bypass, band and revision.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:405:> There is no single Perth price. Total costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess and waiting periods. Any range or estimate should be discussed at consultation with an itemised breakdown; ask your insurer which benefits and gaps apply before booking.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:425:> Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:437:> Appointments take place at the confirmed OSWA clinic locations, and surgery takes place at the hospital selected with your surgeon. Hospital names and affiliations must be verified before publication; the team should confirm the location, admission process and follow-up arrangements during planning.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:23:> Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, MBS criteria generally describe clinically severe obesity as a BMI of 40 or higher, or a BMI of 35 or higher with a major medical comorbidity. A BMI below those thresholds does not meet the MBS eligibility criteria; your surgeon will consider your full health picture and confirm whether any other clinical or rebate rule applies.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:71:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary and no outcome is guaranteed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:97:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:214:> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:256:> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at the hospital location(s) confirmed by your surgeon. Pre- and post-operative consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:274:> Expected weight loss: 70-80% of excess weight, clinical average; results vary. Time in surgery: about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique. Hospital stay: usually 2-3 nights. Return to most activity: about 3-4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:350:> **What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?** The 30/30 rule usually means avoiding fluids for about 30 minutes before and after meals so you can focus on small portions and slow eating. Your dietitian will confirm whether that timing is right for you after surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:386:> At OSWA, gastric bypass patients are assessed by bariatric surgeons including Dr Harsha Chandraratna (MBBS, FRACS) and Dr Stephen Watson (MBBS, FRACS), who perform keyhole bariatric procedures and guide patients through suitability, risks, preparation and follow-up.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:406:> Use our [am I eligible for weight loss surgery guide](/am-i-eligible) to calculate your BMI and understand the other factors your surgeon will consider.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:434:> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at the hospital location(s) confirmed by your surgeon. Pre- and post-operative consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:480:> You may need to follow a specific pre-operative diet for 2-4 weeks to reduce liver size and surgical risk, with guidance from the OSWA dietitian team.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:47:Why it matters: TGA treats direct and indirect prescription-class references in public health-service advertising as prohibited, and the writer followed the brief's medical-weight-management direction as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:87:> Procedures are performed at the Perth hospital locations confirmed by your surgeon, and your team will explain where your procedure and follow-up care would take place during assessment.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:188:> Care from bariatric surgeons with relevant specialist credentials, with procedures performed at Perth hospital locations confirmed with your surgeon. Verify the credentials and hospital affiliations before publication.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:264:> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35–39.9, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility below BMI 35. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:327:> **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?** Some people are not suitable for surgery straight away, or may not be suitable at all. Reasons can include a medical condition that makes anaesthetic or surgery too risky, pregnancy, active substance misuse, an untreated eating disorder or difficulty safely following the required aftercare. Your surgeon will explain the assessment and discuss non-surgical options where appropriate.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:329:> **8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?** There is no single Perth price. Costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess, waiting periods and individual circumstances. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, with an itemised breakdown before you decide.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:333:> **10. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?** Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:173:> **2. How much weight will I actually lose, and what can happen 10 years after surgery?** Average results are reported as around 60–70% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary. Long-term results also vary: some people maintain meaningful weight loss, while others regain some weight over the years; nutrition, reflux, medical conditions, eating patterns, activity and follow-up can all matter. Ongoing clinical review and blood tests help identify issues and guide care.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:181:> Medically reviewed by Dr Harsha Chandraratna, MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric & Upper GI Surgeon, and Dr Stephen Watson, MBBS, FRACS, Bariatric Surgeon; last updated July 2026.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:205:> No intestinal rerouting; food travels through the digestive tract in its normal order, so the sleeve is less malabsorptive than a bypass, although vitamin and mineral monitoring is still needed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:111:   > Eligibility is assessed individually. For a Medicare-listed bariatric surgery pathway, the relevant MBS thresholds are BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity; a BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Your surgeon must assess the clinical options, risks and suitability for your circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:126:   > The MBS thresholds are BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity. A person below BMI 35 does not meet that MBS threshold; a person at BMI 35-39.9 needs the relevant major co-morbidity. Clinical suitability also depends on medical history, surgical risk, readiness for follow-up and individual clinician assessment.
